## Proof

**Setup.** In linearized gravity we write $g_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\nu}+h_{\mu\nu}$ with $|h_{\mu\nu}|\ll 1$, and define the trace $h=\eta^{\mu\nu}h_{\mu\nu}$. The trace-reversed perturbation is
$$\tilde h_{\mu\nu}=h_{\mu\nu}-\tfrac12 h\,\eta_{\mu\nu}.$$
Taking the trace gives $\tilde h = h-\tfrac12 h\cdot 4 = -h$, so $h=-\tilde h$ and the inverse relation is
$$h_{\mu\nu}=\tilde h_{\mu\nu}-\tfrac12\tilde h\,\eta_{\mu\nu}.$$

**Linearized Einstein tensor.** The standard expression is
$$2G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\rho\partial_\mu h^\rho_{\ \nu}+\partial_\rho\partial_\nu h^\rho_{\ \mu}-\Box h_{\mu\nu}-\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h-\eta_{\mu\nu}\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma h^{\rho\sigma}+\eta_{\mu\nu}\Box h,$$
where $\Box=\eta^{\rho\sigma}\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma$.

**Substitution.** Replacing every occurrence of $h_{\mu\nu}$ and $h$ using $h_{\mu\nu}=\tilde h_{\mu\nu}-\tfrac12\tilde h\,\eta_{\mu\nu}$ and $h=-\tilde h$:

1. $\partial_\rho\partial_\mu h^\rho_{\ \nu}=\partial_\rho\partial_\mu\tilde h^\rho_{\ \nu}-\tfrac12\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\tilde h$
2. $\partial_\rho\partial_\nu h^\rho_{\ \mu}=\partial_\rho\partial_\nu\tilde h^\rho_{\ \mu}-\tfrac12\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\tilde h$
3. $\Box h_{\mu\nu}=\Box\tilde h_{\mu\nu}-\tfrac12\eta_{\mu\nu}\Box\tilde h$
4. $\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h=-\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\tilde h$
5. $\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma h^{\rho\sigma}=\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma\tilde h^{\rho\sigma}-\tfrac12\Box\tilde h$
6. $\Box h=-\Box\tilde h$

Collecting the $\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\tilde h$ terms: $-\tfrac12-\tfrac12+1=0$ (they cancel).
Collecting the $\eta_{\mu\nu}\Box\tilde h$ terms: $+\tfrac12+\tfrac12-1=0$ (they cancel).

What remains is
$$2G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\rho\partial_\mu\tilde h^\rho_{\ \nu}+\partial_\rho\partial_\nu\tilde h^\rho_{\ \mu}-\Box\tilde h_{\mu\nu}-\eta_{\mu\nu}\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma\tilde h^{\rho\sigma},$$
or equivalently
$$G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=-\tfrac12\Box\tilde h_{\mu\nu}+\tfrac12\partial_\rho\partial_{(\mu}\tilde h^\rho_{\ \nu)}-\tfrac12\eta_{\mu\nu}\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma\tilde h^{\rho\sigma}.$$

**Applying the Lorenz gauge.** The Lorenz (harmonic) gauge condition is
$$\partial_\mu\tilde h^{\mu\nu}=0.$$
This immediately kills each of the three derivative terms:
- $\partial_\rho\tilde h^\rho_{\ \nu}=\eta_{\nu\sigma}\partial_\rho\tilde h^{\rho\sigma}=0$,
- $\partial_\rho\tilde h^\rho_{\ \mu}=\eta_{\mu\sigma}\partial_\rho\tilde h^{\rho\sigma}=0$,
- $\partial_\rho\partial_\sigma\tilde h^{\rho\sigma}=\partial_\sigma(\partial_\rho\tilde h^{\rho\sigma})=0$.

**Result.** All the gauge-dependent terms drop out, leaving the simple wave equation
$$\boxed{\,G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=-\tfrac12\,\Box\,\tilde h_{\mu\nu}\,}.$$

Combined with the Einstein equation $G^{(1)}_{\mu\nu}=8\pi G\,T_{\mu\nu}$, this yields the inhomogeneous wave equation $\Box\tilde h_{\mu\nu}=-16\pi G\,T_{\mu\nu}$, confirming that under the Lorenz gauge the trace-reversed perturbation propagates as a standard wave sourced by the stress-energy tensor.

